Most codebases already contain patterns nobody named. Spotting them is a skill worth practicing before you start reaching for new ones.

```python
def make_parser(file_type):
    if file_type == "csv":
        return CsvParser()
    elif file_type == "json":
        return JsonParser()
    raise ValueError(file_type)
```

This function is already a **Factory Method** in everything but name — it centralizes "which concrete class do I build" behind one call site. Naming it doesn't change the code; it lets you talk about extending it ("should this become a registered-factory map instead of an if/elif chain?") using shared vocabulary instead of re-explaining the whole function.
